// The gate `block_escape.c` said was MASKED, now that it can be built.
//
// handle_go_call marks the CALLEE's taint, not just the arguments, so a
// `go p.m()` inside an arena block marks `p` and forces the site to the heap.
// That fix carried this note:
//
//   Currently masked by a SEPARATE defect (`go p.m()` on a pointer receiver
//   fails module verification, so the program does not build)
//
// So the analysis was correct and had no end-to-end test, because no program
// of this shape compiled. The `go` method-callee fix removes that defect, and
// this probe is the test the note was waiting for.
//
// `stash` keeps its receiver in a package global, and `kept` is read AFTER
// the arena block frees its region. Without the callee-taint marking the
// site is arena-eligible, and that read is a use-after-free.
//
// TWO SHAPE CHOICES, both load-bearing — the first version of this probe had
// neither and could NOT fail:
//
//   1. `T` holds NO channel. A struct with a channel field is forced to the
//      heap for an unrelated reason, which masked the very thing being
//      tested: the site stayed on the heap even with the marking removed.
//      Verified by mutation — with `T` channel-free, removing the
//      mark_escapes line in handle_go_call flips the site to
//      goo_arena_alloc, and restoring it flips it back.
//   2. The channel is a PACKAGE GLOBAL, not a field and not an argument.
//      `go f(args)` on a function value is a separate v1 restriction, and a
//      channel field would reintroduce problem 1.
package main

type T struct {
	x int
}

var kept *T
var done chan int

func (t *T) stash() {
	kept = t
	done <- 1
}

func main() {
	done = make(chan int)
	arena {
		p := &T{x: 9}
		go p.stash()
		<-done
	}
	println(kept.x)
}
